<section class="inner-sec">
  <div class="sec-eyebrow">How it works in a practice</div>
  <h2>Every call answered, every chair worth filling</h2>
  <p class="lede">One receptionist covers the phone, the texts and the website — catching new patients, reactivating overdue ones, and handling the scheduling churn your desk currently absorbs by hand.</p>
  <div class="svc-grid">
    <div class="svc"><strong>Missed-call text-back</strong>A call nobody could reach becomes a text conversation in seconds, including on the days and hours the office is closed.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>The emergency call, handled correctly</strong>It recognises urgency, gives your posted after-hours instructions, and gets the patient onto the first available slot. It never assesses the problem.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Works the hygiene recall</strong>Overdue patients get a friendly text that books them, instead of a call your desk never gets to. The list finally gets worked.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Fills short-notice openings</strong>When a slot opens, it offers it out and takes the first yes — so the chair gets filled without anyone dialling down a list.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Answers the front-desk questions</strong>Hours, location, whether you're taking new patients, which plans you're in-network with, what a first visit involves — answered instantly, any hour.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Human handoff</strong>Anything clinical, anything about a specific treatment plan, anything sensitive goes to your team with a summary of the conversation.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="inner-sec" style="padding-top: 10px;">
  <div class="sec-eyebrow">Who it's built for</div>
  <h2>However the practice is set up</h2>
  <p class="lede">The front-desk load is different in a single-doctor office than in a group practice, and the receptionist is configured to match rather than assuming one shape.</p>
  <div class="svc-grid">
    <div class="svc"><strong>Single-doctor practices</strong>One desk, one schedule, and no cover when that person is at lunch, on another line, or off for the day.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Group &amp; multi-provider</strong>More chairs means more churn — reschedules, recalls and new-patient calls arriving faster than one desk can work them.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Practices you just bought</strong>A new owner inherits a patient list and often a neglected recall list. That list is the fastest revenue in the building.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Hygiene-driven schedules</strong>If cleanings anchor the calendar, keeping recall current is the whole game — and it's the first task to get dropped.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Pediatric &amp; family</strong>Parents book after work and on weekends, exactly when the office is closed and the call goes unanswered.</div>
    <div class="svc"><strong>Cosmetic &amp; implant cases</strong>High-value inquiries deserve a real response the same day, not a callback slot two days out.</div>
  </div>
</section>

