<section class="inner-sec" style="padding-top: 10px;">
  <div class="proof-band">
    <div class="sec-eyebrow">Where the line is</div>
    <h2>It schedules. It never assesses.</h2>
    <p>Everything a dental front desk does that isn't clinical — answering, scheduling, reminding, reactivating — is work this can take. Everything that touches a diagnosis, a treatment plan, a chart or someone's coverage stays with your team, because a confident wrong answer in any of those costs far more than a missed call.</p>
    <div class="proof-points">
      <div class="ppoint">
        <strong>It never assesses a problem</strong>
        <span>A patient describing pain gets your posted urgency instructions and the first available slot — never an opinion on what's wrong or how bad it is.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ppoint">
        <strong>No treatment prices</strong>
        <span>It shares what you've published — new-patient exam, cleaning, whether you offer financing — and nothing else. Treatment estimates come from your team, after an exam.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ppoint">
        <strong>It never verifies benefits</strong>
        <span>It can say which plans you're in-network with. What a specific patient's plan actually covers reaches a human, because a wrong answer there becomes a billing dispute.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ppoint">
        <strong>It never opens a chart</strong>
        <span>Front-desk logistics only — a name, a number, a reason to follow up. No records, no history, which is why most practices need no compliance layer at all.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

